WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:05.589
Whether you're a lifelong horror hound or
a new convert to the joys of the genre, it's

00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:07.320
a good time to be a horror fan.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.200
Recent years have had something for everyone,
and 2019 is showing no sign of letting us

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:12.700
down yet.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.820
Here are the best horror films of the year
so far.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.820
Midsommar follows Dani, a young girl tagging
along with her boyfriend and his friends to

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.890
a remote Swedish village as it prepares for
a rare midsummer festival.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.020
As you might expect, things escalate from
there.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.220
Midsommar isn't just horrifying — while
there are some standard horror elements thrown

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:37.000
in, the bulk of what makes the movie frightening
is also what it has in common with Hereditary.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.720
It's about guilt and grief and loss, and the
entire film feels raw and unnerving.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:47.180
This tale of a family encountering their own
crazed, murderous doubles is even more layered

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:53.070
and complex in its themes than Jordan Peele's
previous Get Out, and decidedly more ambitious.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.570
It's intense, deeply original, and scary as
heck.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.500
Us proves that Jordan Peele's position as
one of the most important creative voices

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.399
of his generation is secure.

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:07.490
Plus, the movie also proves definitively that
Lupita Nyong'o is one of the greatest actors

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:08.610
currently working.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.760
"The girl had a second child, a boy this time.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.900
They had to cut her open and take him from
her belly."

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.659
The original Happy Death Day finds a college
student getting murdered by a masked killer

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:26.740
again and again, forced to relive her birthday
until she can figure out how to break the

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:27.240
time loop.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:33.300
2019's sequel, Happy Death Day 2U moves the
story just one day into the future and deeper

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.420
into science fiction.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.460
The film is packed with quantum reactors,
alternate timelines, and colliding doppelgangers

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.420
twisting in all kinds of directions, including
back into the original film.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.810
It's a bigger, better, wilder film than the
original.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.250
Reboots can be tricky.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:54.139
Follow too closely to the original, and there's
a good chance a film will be labeled as lazy.

00:01:54.139 --> 00:01:57.630
Go too far outside the lines, you'll lose
the nostalgia appeal.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:03.149
2019's Child's Play manges to walk that fine
line, giving a 1980s horror story about a

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:08.060
possessed doll an internet-era update that
plays on the ridiculousness of its premise.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.940
Yes, Child's Play is going to be about what
you'd expect to see from a killer doll film.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.870
But it also seems to have an understanding
of its own limitations and doesn't try to

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.790
project some deeper meaning.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:22.820
It's an hour and a half of gory horror, wrapped
up in a bow of '80s nostalgia.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:28.710
Velvet Buzzsaw finds director Dan Gilroy turning
a satirical lens on the pretentious culture

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:29.360
of fine art.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:34.620
Jake Gyllenhaal and Rene Russo lead an ensemble
cast of characters who come into contact with

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.780
the works of a little-known deceased painter.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.579
The paintings themselves begin killing off
critics and dealers one by one, putting a

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.540
serious crimp in their lucrative dealings
and torrid affairs.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.420
This supernatural, occasionally silly, blood-drenched
commentary on the collision of art and commerce

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.440
didn't quite garner the overwhelming praise
that Nightcrawler earned.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:58.200
Nonetheless, plenty of critics found plenty
to enjoy in this campy horror flick.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:03.620
"The best work is only enjoyed by a few and
they buy what they're told, so why not join

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:04.620
the party?"

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.620
Fresh off Jordan Peele's 2017 horror hit Get
Out, Allison Williams takes another dive into

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:14.240
the genre here as Charlotte, a former musical
prodigy who begins an affair with her school's

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.000
current lead cellist, Elizabeth.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:20.200
While it's clear from the opening credits
of The Perfection that Charlotte doesn't have

00:03:20.219 --> 00:03:24.959
the purest of intentions, the full extent
of her motivation makes for one hell of a

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:25.460
twisted ride.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.870
To say more would spoil the insane twists
of the film, but trust us when we say that

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:31.920
the film is not for the faint of heart.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.340
The film itself might not be perfect, but
The Perfection definitely feels one of a kind.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.200
Greta is as much about emotional vulnerability
as it is about the horrors of befriending

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.020
random strangers in the New York City subway
system.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:48.579
Chloë Grace Moretz plays Frances, a young
woman new to the city, who's dealing with

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.000
her mother's recent death and is in need of
a friend.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.260
Enter Greta, a seemingly kind older woman
who's forgotten her purse on the subway and

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:57.200
could also use a friend.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.140
The pair strike up a relationship and it isn't
nearly as innocent as it first appears to

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:02.640
be.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:08.280
The wide open spaces of the American frontier
are often romanticized by classic westerns,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.980
but The Wind recognizes the horrific potential
of all that emptiness.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.060
In the film, tough frontierswoman Lizzy is
haunted by that emptiness, personified by

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.820
the ever-present wind howling across the plains.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.790
Despite reassurances from her husband, Lizzy
becomes increasingly convinced that some sort

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:27.320
of demonic presence is haunting their homestead.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:31.480
Is there something supernatural out there,
or is the horror of The Wind nothing more

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.960
or less than loneliness itself?

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.160
It's a question that will haunt anyone who
goes along for this moody, atmospheric ride.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.530
The Hole in the Ground spins a subtle story
about a woman and her son moving to a village

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.730
in the countryside to escape a troubled past.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:59.220
But there's something disturbing about their
new home — namely, the enormous sinkhole

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:00.340
in the nearby woods.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.920
The atmosphere and metaphors of The Hole in
the Ground are far better experienced than

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:08.861
described, but we'll just say that you won't
be disappointed when you dive in to check

00:05:08.861 --> 00:05:09.840
them out for yourself.

